[2017-09-19T08:09:13Z | goodchip | comment]
Thank you for your answers,

@henrikekblad: I put a repeater in debug mode, and actually the frame seems well corrumpuous at the source.

@fallberg & @tekka007: the lenght field is always equal to the actual length of the payload (it is precisely on this parameter that my code is based to detect a corrupted frame). In short, the problem does not seem to come at all from the RF protocol.

So I disconnected all repeaters and the problem remains.

I suspected many repeaters because the problem occurred when I installed them.

I have refocused on the gateway, which seems to be the common denominator: by plugging a MySensors key (serial gateway on USB via FTDI) the problem disappears completely.

I suspect actually the serial link between RPi and the ATMEGA328p.

I look at that and keep you informed.

Best regards

[2017-09-20T09:04:53Z | goodchip | comment]
Origin of the problem solved!

**It was a write too fast on the serial port** (C POSIX with RPi running Raspbian).

Would not it be wise to check the ID transferred in the payload?

If you do not see the interest, I can close this issue.

[2017-09-20T09:38:22Z | goodchip | comment]
For information, here is the new routine I use (with usleep):

```
#define SERIAL_TRANSMISSION_LENGTH 10      // 8N1 = 10 bits
#define SERIAL_BAUDRATE 38400

writeSerial( const char* string ) {

    int myReturn = 0;

    serialMutex.lock();
    lastMutexLocked = MUTEX_WRITE;

    size_t myLength = strlen( string );

    myWriteLength = write( fileDescriptor, string, myLength );

    // waiting the end of wrote frame:
    usleep( (unsigned int)( ceil( 1000 * ( myWriteLength * (SERIAL_TRANSMISSION_LENGTH) ) / (double)(SERIAL_BAUDRATE) ) ) );

    if( myWriteLength != myLength ) {
        myReturn = false;
    }

    serialMutex.unlock();

}
```

